WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.534
[music playing]

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.136
NARRATOR: In the
desert of Gebelein,

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:06.673
just south of Luxor,
Meredith searches

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.844
for evidence of Egypt's
earliest death rites.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.247
She believes the myths that
drove Egyptians to mummify

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:17.350
their own bodies had
roots much earlier than

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:19.486
ancient Egyptian civilization.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:26.326
Prehistoric rock art
depicting wildlife

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:30.497
suggests this area was
once a sacred place.

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:34.701
In the 19th century,
archeologists identified it

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:37.170
as a burial ground.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:40.440
This is a desolate
dry landscape.

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:43.910
Nothing much to
see on the surface.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:46.146
But underneath these
rocks and sand,

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:50.150
there would have been
prehistoric tombs.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.253
NARRATOR: In 1896,
an Egyptologist

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:56.890
from the British
Museum received a tip

00:00:56.956 --> 00:01:00.693
off of a momentous discovery.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.097
Buried under a thin
layer of desert debris,

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:08.067
local farmers had found a
mummified body preserved

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:10.670
in astonishing detail.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.940
On the upper arm,
tattoos of horned animals

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:15.708
were still visible.

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:19.712
And from the head sprouted
tufts of red hair,

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:23.249
earning this mummy its
original nickname, ginger.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:28.755
There was a puncture wound
to the left shoulder blade

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.691
and a fractured rib beneath.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:34.894
Forensic analysis has
revealed the body is

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:38.565
male and over 5,000 years old.

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:41.634
But how is this body
so perfectly preserved?

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:48.007
For Meredith, the evidence
suggests the man's

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:50.443
mummification was not planned.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.214
Instead, it was an
accident of nature.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.615
In this photo,
I can see there's

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:57.817
no bandages or
anything that would

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:59.385
have preserved this mummy.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:00.887
So it would seem
that this was not

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.189
an intentional mummification.

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.358
He was preserved by the sand.

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:08.795
There was no ritual or magic
or anything involved, just

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:10.129
the drying power of nature.

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:15.635
NARRATOR: Hundreds of years
before the first pharaoh,

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:18.238
this man lived around
the fertile flood

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:19.806
plains along the River Nile.

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:26.546
But around his 20th birthday,
he was brutally stabbed

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:30.383
in the back and left for dead.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.054
He was buried in a shallow grave
with simple clay grave goods

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:38.157
and covered with sand.

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.760
But the desert sun
and hot dry sand

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:44.731
quickly evaporated all
the water from his body,

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.433
stopping it from
decomposing, freezing

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:56.609
him in time until his
discovery 5,400 years later.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.812
Archeologists have found
no papyri that explained

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:02.582
the historical origins
of mummification,

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:05.752
but Meredith believes the
practice could have evolved

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.520
from accidents of nature.

00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:09.689
Natural mummies
like the Gebelein man

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.491
would have inspired
ancient Egyptians.

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:13.192
That if they could
use technology

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:16.195
to harness the power of
nature, their bodies too could

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.665
last forever into eternity.

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:21.968
It's a truly
spectacular discovery.

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:24.971
It tells us where
mummification started.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:28.741
[music playing]

